<ArticleLayout
  title="What Is Artisanal & Small-Scale Mining?"
  description="A plain introduction to ASM: who mines this way, why it matters to formal supply chains, and how it differs from large-scale mining."
>
  <p>
    Artisanal and small-scale mining, usually shortened to ASM, covers
    mineral extraction that relies on manual labour and simple tools.
    It sits at the opposite end of the spectrum from large-scale mining
    (LSM), which operates through formal concessions, mechanised
    equipment and corporate reporting structures. ASM is often informal
    or semi-formal, organised around individual miners, families or
    small cooperatives.
  </p>
  <p>
    The term covers a wide range of realities. It includes gold panning
    in riverbeds, shaft mining for diamonds and gemstones, and
    small-scale extraction of minerals like tin, tantalum and tungsten.
    Scale and organisation define the category more than any specific
    mineral: an ASM operation can be highly skilled and productive while
    still lacking the formal registration, safety systems or
    documentation that a buyer or regulator expects.
  </p>

  <p>
    ASM sites also rarely sit outside existing social structures.
    Customary land tenure and traditional authorities, chiefs, elders and
    community leadership bodies, typically govern who can mine a given
    piece of ground well before any formal licence enters the picture,
    and community structures around an ASM site often predate the mining
    itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Why ASM matters to formal supply chains</h2>
  <p>
    The scale involved makes ASM hard to treat as a side issue. The
    World Bank and Pact estimate that around 45 million people work
    directly in ASM across roughly 80 countries, making it the largest
    mining workforce of any kind, and industry estimates put ASM&rsquo;s
    share of global gold production at roughly 15 to 20 percent. Material
    from artisanal sources enters the same markets as material from
    industrial mines, often after passing through several intermediaries
    who may or may not document its origin carefully.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is exactly where responsible sourcing frameworks like the OECD
    Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals
    from Conflict-Affected and High-Risk Areas come in: they ask buyers
    to understand and document where material actually came from, even
    when the source is informal.
  </p>

  <h2>Where ASM struggles, and where advisory work helps</h2>
  <p>
    The practical challenges facing ASM operators are rarely about
    willingness. Most operators want safer sites, better prices and
    recognised status; what they usually lack is a clear, affordable
    path to get there; formalisation processes that are slow or costly,
    limited access to the record-keeping and safety practices buyers
    ask for, and little leverage to negotiate fair terms with
    intermediaries.
  </p>
  <p>
    That gap between where an ASM operation stands today and what a
    formal buyer or investor expects is the space Orivia works in,
    informed by direct experience running an ASM operation ourselves.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
